  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 242 PM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Showers are becoming more widespread early this \nafternoon, particularly across the northern Adirondacks, across Lake \nChamplain and into northern Vermont. This activity is associated \nwith an upper level trough that is swinging across eastern Ontario \nand into southern Quebec. Some of the more robust showers this \nafternoon could produce a few rumbles of thunder along with some \nlocally gusty winds to maybe 30 mph or so, but nothing strong/severe \nis anticipated. Showers will wane in coverage with sunset, except \ndownwind of Lake Ontario, where W/SW flow over the relatively warm \nwater will allow for lake enhanced showers to linger overnight. The \nbulk of the moisture, cloud cover, and precipitation will remain \nacross NY. Vermont should see at least some clearing overnight, \nwhich should allow river valley fog to form again tonight/early \nTuesday. Lows tonight will be comfortable, mainly in the 50s, \nthough some of the more sheltered locations could drop into the \n40s. \n\nThe upper trough will slowly slide along the international border on \nTuesday, providing lift for another round of scattered showers. \nCoverage and intensity should be less than today, and any \nthunderstorms fairly isolated. Winds will turn to the northwest \nduring the afternoon once the trough axis moves to our east, \nushering in some drier air. Highs will generally be in the 70s, \nthough some spots in the Adirondacks and Northeast Kingdom may \nremain in the 60s. After another comfortable night Tuesday night, \nWednesday should be a pleasant day. Clouds will develop during the \nafternoon, but no rain is expected. Highs will be in the 70s to \naround 80F.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Late this week, upper level troughing will rotate across \neastern Canada, anchored over Hudson Bay, sending a surface low \nthrough Quebec and its associated frontal boundaries swinging across \nnorthern New York and Vermont Thursday afternoon through Friday. \nChances of measurable precipitation run around 60-90% during this \ntime frame, highest Thursday afternoon and evening (first round) as \nwell as Friday morning and afternoon (second round and stronger cold \nfront). Thursday is looking like the warmest day of the week for \nmost of us as highs reach the upper 70s and lower 80s in southerly \nflow. Thursday night should also be mild as southerly flow continues \nwith lows in the mid 50s to mid 60s. Friday will be a touch cooler \nbut still relatively warm with highs in the 70s and lower 80s. Then, \nfollowing the passage of the cold front, we see lows drop into the \n40s to mid 50s over the weekend as showers come to an end Friday \nnight. Deterministic guidance consensus also diverges over the \nweekend, so changes to this forecast are possible over the next few \ndays. At the moment, upper cyclonic flow continues over Canada while \nsurface high pressure builds in over the northeastern US, so weekend \nhighs are likely to be seasonable in the 70s, though a few low 80s \nin the southern and wider valleys are possible. High pressure looks \nto start breaking down Sunday or Sunday night, resulting in the \nreturn of shower chances heading into the start of the next work \nweek as the trough shifts into the Canadian maritimes, dragging a \ncold front towards the Northeast. There remains a wide range of \npossible high temperatures on Monday, anywhere from the 60s to lower \n80s. Current forecast leans on the warmer side, in line with \nensemble and blended guidance, but there is the potential for \nconditions to be quite a bit cooler if we're to believe the \ndeterministic global models.",
